From f68e90b01b856c30c5c2691950ff1eaf24e103d4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arien Judge <arien.judge@morsemicro.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 10:07:02 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] supplicant: manage HaLow interfaces through
 wpa_supplicant_s1g

Morse Micro HaLow are handled by wpa_supplicant_s1g. As a modified
wpa_supplicant, it can be configured such that its DBus name,
object path and interface prefix are distinct to the standard
wpa_supplicant allowing the use of the standard supplicant for non-s1g
alongside the Morse Micro supplicant for Wi-Fi HaLow.
NetworkManager expects only one supplicant, so needs further modification
to communicate concurrently with regular Wi-Fi and HaLow radios.

All NMSupplicantManager per-supplicant state (name owner, capabilities,
D-Bus subscriptions) are moved into a new SupplInst, so that existing
implementation functions against either supplicant. NMSupplicantManager
holds two SupplInst, one for wpa_supplicant and one for wpa_supplicant_s1g.

Interface creation picks the instance when the request is made based on
the driver name. When /sys/class/net/<ifname>/device/driver points at a
"morse" the interface is directed to wpa_supplicant_s1g.

nm_supplicant_manager_is_available() reports available if either
supplicant is up.

nm_supplicant_manager_set_wfd_ies() sets WFDIEs on every running
supplicant.

After creation actions are determined by the DBus path. This therefore
asserts that the wpa_supplicant_s1g used for "morse" drivers must be
available on a /fi/w1/wpa_supplicant_s1g path.

NMSupplicantInterface caches the name owner and capabilities from the
object path at construction. For all subsequent DBus calls, signals, properties
and errors it determines if it needs to use an S1G variant or standard.

Based-on-patch-by: Chetan Mistry <chetan.mistry@morsemicro.com>
Signed-off-by: Arien Judge <arien.judge@morsemicro.com>
